  "query_preview": "..\n\nA tight upper low dropping into Arizona will produce a swath of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and evening,\nmainly through Cochise, Graham, and Greenlee counties. A modest\nbut unseasonably moist air mass characterized by precipitable \nwater to around 0.8 inches was overspread across southeastern \nArizona this afternoon, responsible for MUCAPE values of to near\n1000 J/kg. This atmospheric setup will be adequate for isolated\nmoderate rainfall rates with a low but non-zero threat of flash\nflooding, if perhaps a slower moving storm sits over a location\nor multiple cells pass over the same area. Tucson and eastern \nPinal look to remain on the western periphery of this potential,\nwith 10-20 percent chances through this evening. A couple \nstronger storms with strong wind gusts and small hail will be \npossible, mainly in Cochise and southern Graham/Greenlee. \nThunderstorm activity will generally diminish in the evening, \nbut the nearby upper low will keep shower chances going in \neastern areas through early Saturday morning. A few additional \nSaturday afternoon showers will be possible in southern Cochise \nand Santa Cruz as cold temperatures aloft from the crossing \nupper low will produce weak instability until daytime heating is\nlost.\n\nBreezy easterly winds are expected Saturday and Sunday as a\nsurface pressure gradient tightens under the exiting upper low.\nWinds of 12-20 mph with gusts to 30 mph are generally forecast,\nwith winds a bit stronger in areas open to easterly flow.\n\nMean flow aloft will generally favor a ridging pattern this\nweek, though a few weak passing dry lows will keep temperatures\nnear or below normal through the forecast period. By the end of\nthe week ensemble models continue to produce lower height \nanomalies, indicative of a pattern that may allow precipitation \nchances to return to the forecast. Strong uncertainty in any \ndetails remain however.",
